Chapter: ProbeH2LambdaTypes
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 H2's matrix: what type the IR wire carries for a lambda parameter, one
 case per shape, so an answer localizes instead of confirming a mood.

 Every case answers a number and bare metal is the oracle; the answers are
 banked in `probe-h2-lambda-types.expected` beside this file, because a
 compile that is never RUN throws away the run's only correctness signal.
 The second half is the WIRE: compile in `IR-CCE` mode and read the
 `(param "<name>" ...)` cells of the lifted `__lam_N` definitions.

 REBUILT 2026-08-27 after a cold read took the first version apart. What
 that read established, from compiler source and with no machine time, is
 that the `error` cells are manufactured in LOWERING, not by the checker:
 a `let` lowers its bound value with expected type `ErrorTy`
 (`IR/Lowering.codex:689`), `lower-lambda` peels its parameters off that
 expected type (`:722-724`), `peel-fun-param` answers `ErrorTy` for any
 non-`FunTy` (`Types/CodexTypeHelpers.codex:4-10`), and the unifier's own
 answer -- which exists, and which `deep-resolve` would give -- is never
 asked for. So the matrix is no longer trying to establish THAT. It is
 here to bound the fix: which shapes are affected, and which candidate
 repair would be a real recovery rather than a lucky default.

 Four things the first version got wrong, all fixed here:

   - Its "control" differed from the case it controlled in five respects
     and its prose misnamed the variable. Cases b and e are genuine
     one-respect controls now, each paired with the case above it.
   - It had no `.expected` and its instrument never booted the image, so
     the numbers it claimed as the oracle were never read.
   - Every case's true answer was `Integer`, which is also the language's
     default -- so a repair that simply defaulted an `error` parameter to
     `Integer` would have passed the whole matrix. Case f is `Text`.
   - It had no case where the type is genuinely unknowable, so falsifier 2
     (is a diagnostic being discarded) had nothing to be about. Case g is
     that case.

 A control PAIR agrees on its number by design -- a and b both answer 3, d
 and e both answer 7 -- because the pair is meant to differ on the wire and
 nowhere else. A wrong line is still identified by its position.

Section: Functions

  twice : (Integer -> Integer), Integer -> Integer
  twice (g) (x) = g (g x)

  d-recv : Integer, (Integer -> Integer) -> Integer
  d-recv (base) (step) = twice step base

  give-list : (Integer -> List Integer), Integer -> List Integer
  give-list (mk) (i) = mk i

 a. A lambda capturing a LIST, bound by `let`, parameter unused.
 `roc-closure-captures-list` reduced. The CAPTURE arrives typed -- it is
 collected from its use by a different pass entirely
 (`IR/LambdaLifting.codex:288-296`) -- and the lambda's own parameter is
 the cell in question.

  case-a : Integer -> Integer
  case-a (n) =
   let xs = [n, n + 1]
   in let f = \i -> xs
   in list-at (f 0) 0 + list-at (f 0) 1

 b. CONTROL FOR a. The same capturing lambda, the same body, the same
 answer; the one respect that differs is that it sits at a DECLARED
 parameter, so an expected `FunTy` reaches `lower-lambda` instead of the
 `ErrorTy` a `let` hands down. This is the only case that isolates the
 capture path from the parameter path.

  case-b : Integer -> Integer
  case-b (n) =
   let xs = [n, n + 1]
   in let got = give-list (\i -> xs) 0
   in list-at got 0 + list-at got 1

 c. The parameter is USED. The body reads `(name "i" int-default)` while
 the signature one node up says `error`, in the same definition. The
 first version also carried a variant with an unused parameter and an
 integer body; it was the same call site twice and it is gone.

  case-c : Integer -> Integer
  case-c (n) =
   let f = \i -> i
   in f n

 d. `roc-fold-sum`'s shape reduced: a lambda LITERAL in function position,
 applied on the spot, taking another lambda literal as an argument. Two
 parameters of one lifted definition arriving differently -- `base` from
 the argument `n`, `step` from a literal that has no type of its own yet.

  case-d : Integer -> Integer
  case-d (n) = (\base step -> twice step base) n (\y -> y + 1)

 e. CONTROL FOR d. The same literal `\y -> y + 1`, in the same second
 argument position, reaching the same `twice` through the same body, for
 the same answer. The one respect that differs is that the receiver is a
 DECLARED definition rather than a lambda literal. If d is dirty and e is
 clean, the difference is attributable to that and to nothing else.

  case-e : Integer -> Integer
  case-e (n) = d-recv n (\y -> y + 1)

 f. TEXT, so that a repair which defaults an `error` parameter to
 `Integer` fails visibly instead of passing. Every other case's true
 answer is `Integer`, which is also the language's default, and a matrix
 that cannot tell a recovered type from a lucky guess is not measuring the
 repair it exists to bound.

  case-f : Text -> Text
  case-f (t) =
   let g = \s -> s & "!"
   in g t

 g. Bound and NEVER APPLIED, so nothing anywhere constrains `k`. This is
 the only case where `error` would be an honest answer rather than a
 discarded one, and it is what falsifier 2 is about: if the compiler files
 a diagnostic for any case here, this is the case it should be about, and
 a diagnostic on the others would mean we are throwing one away.

  case-g : Integer -> Integer
  case-g (n) =
   let h = \k -> 1
   in n

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (case-a 1))
   print-line-uni (show (case-b 1))
   print-line-uni (show (case-c 9))
   print-line-uni (show (case-d 5))
   print-line-uni (show (case-e 5))
   print-line-uni (case-f "x")
   print-line-uni (show (case-g 4))
  end
